            <ArticleTitle>Fracture of zotarolimus-eluting stent after implantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>618-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Drug-eluting stents were developed and approved for the reduction of in-stent restenosis. However, restenosis still occurs, and stent fracture is suggested as a cause of restenosis after implantation. Although sirolimus-eluting stents are considered to carry a high risk of fracture, the risk is also present with other drug-eluting stents. Herein, we report the case of a 78-year-old woman who received a zotarolimus-eluting stent for a bifurcation lesion of the left anterior descending coronary artery. Ten months later, she underwent coronary angiography due to angina. The angiogram revealed in-stent restenosis, with a grade IV stent fracture. After percutaneous coronary angioplasty, the patient's clinical symptoms improved.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
